Cell["\<\
h[k] gives a Hermitian Hamiltonian and velocities[k] gives its \
derivative-matrix list; alternatively hv[k] returns both as {h[k], \
velocities[k]}.\
\>", "Notes",
 CellID->1905300265,ExpressionUUID->"a6a248bb-ca8f-a04d-9f1e-cd14b724a4c5"],

Cell["\<\
dirs defaults to {1, 1}, selecting a diagonal metric component. Its entries \
must be valid one-based velocity indices.\
\>", "Notes",
 CellID->278057687,ExpressionUUID->"1dd1e9e4-6319-3a4f-92b4-5e91da74a209"],

Cell["\<\
An integer filling counts occupied low-energy states; a real filling is a \
Fermi energy. Use a real number such as 0. to distinguish Fermi energy from \
band count.\
\>", "Notes",
 CellID->421631306,ExpressionUUID->"06139386-0ba5-534c-89ca-e2029033c7fb"],

Cell["\<\
Returns the real part of the occupied-to-unoccupied Kubo sum. The optional q \
insertion replaces the first velocity with (q.v + v.q)/2; q -> 1 gives the \
ordinary metric.\
\>", "Notes",
 CellID->842109527,ExpressionUUID->"ab754c86-85a4-bf40-b148-ac08395a83ce"],

Cell["\<\
Empty or completely occupied spectra return 0. Degeneracies across the \
filling require care because energy differences enter the denominator \
squared. Eigensystem options are accepted.\
\>", "Notes",
 CellID->1160400512,ExpressionUUID->"bec0c6d6-ac76-9f4a-b908-7a5586384130"]
}, Closed]],

